he function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b9e5c87-171d-5626-ae59-fa7b69d9a95a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455435 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}-2e \\\\  & =2e({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{3}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22016566-6911-5c05-b305-606b27ad2ee0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455436 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{3})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455443 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ed7acf8-9750-5997-81ee-fb6a8cd2e910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455443 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f1c8267-786a-5d55-8970-d1a062c41c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455445 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50247cc3-8637-5b67-95c3-4cdd3dd3329e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455452 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455453 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50bd506e-5f91-5da4-a6e7-565dbeb70122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455453 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(-1)}{2}-(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455471 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a3675b0-e883-52dd-8e3e-7330cc6b3856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455471 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }$, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta }{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{4}⁡3\\theta }}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76768" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455484 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{x}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c7ce8e8-7ebb-5185-86d9-47c695c84f48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455484 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ because $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}⟹\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{x}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =32{\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}({1}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcf9f8c6-5d3c-5211-90e2-bddb94921272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455490 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy(1-{x}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce434154-3398-5488-977f-b3adb13e5b25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455497 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455508 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee634955-812e-57a4-9691-da172190ae0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455508 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ))}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186455412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455509 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eec86b54-2bc2-5f46-8f9a-0f8c56be4962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186455509 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =27{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}27{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =81\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}((\\frac{1}{2})^{6}-{0}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
